@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@node Why a new document format?
@section Why a new document format?

We already have a lot of plain text document formats and some of them are great at what they do but they all have terrible referencig system and poor scaling. Most plain text document formats use "headlines" (or "heading") as their section title and the sections are later referenced with that title. But, Searching such headline is tiresome for both computers and humans alike. One will have to search through the whole document until they find the the referenced section. This means the longer your document is, the more time it'll take for you to find your desired section.

This is terrible if you want to quickly scrape a section from your long plain text file (for example, if you want to make a todo list out of your plain text document and you want to check whether one item is done or not or if you want to read your 22nd September journal entry from 3 years ago but you have a really long list). Furthermore, there can be more than one section with the same headline title. This will create confusion. To fix these issues Tag uses something similar to Niklas Luhmann's Zettlekasten method. Tag marks each section with a permenant number (tag) and sorts the sections by tag number.

@node How Tag works
@section How Tag works

If we tag our sections with permanent floating point numbers (for example: @code{9.0}) and sort the sections based on those tags, we'll have a plain text document format that's quick to look up and since the sections are permanently tagged, we can use that to reference in other sections. The reason for choosing floating point numbers for tagging is we want to be able to insert a new tag between two old tags without having to change the value of any old tag. For example, let's say we have four sections: @code{@@1.0, @@2.0, @@3.0, @@4.0}.

@example
@@1.0
@@2.0
@@3.0
@@4.0
@end example

@noindent And, we want to insert a new section after @code{@@1.0}. In other numbered document files we'd have to name the new section the new @code{@@2.0} and the old @code{@@2.0} would be the new @code{@@3.0}... But, if we change the already assigned tag numbers, we will would lose the purpose of referencing sections with their respective tag numbers. What we do here is tag the new section a number between @code{@@1.0} and @code{@@2.0}. @code{@@1.1} would suffice here. So, the five sections are @code{@@1.0, @@1.1, @@2.0, @@3.0, @@4.0}. @code{@@1.1} is between @code{@@1.0} and @code{@@2.0} and no section had to change its tag.

@example
@@1.0
@@1.1 <-- the newly inserted tag
@@2.0
@@3.0
@@4.0
@end example

@noindent If we want to insert anything between @code{@@1.0} and @code{@@1.1} then it could be @code{@@1.01} and a new tag between @code{@@1.1} and @code{@@2.0} could be @code{@@1.2}. Note that a tag can be any number that isn't already taken. Since we are using floating point numbers, we theoretically have infinite possibilities to choose from.

@node Tagline
@section Tagline

The line that tags a section and has the tag number, title of the section, section's references, and section's flags is called tagline. It starts with @code{@@} followed by the tag number, title, references, and flags. Example of a tagline:

@example
@@1.5 Butterflies _ @@1.2 ^ incomplete
@end example

This line states that the section tag is @code{@@1.5}, followed by the section title "Butterflies", references section @code{@@1.2}, and it has the flag @code{incomplete} which denotes that the section isn't finished yet. The @code{_} (sub) and @code{^} (sup) signs are respectively used for reference lists and flag lists.

@node Tag numbering
@section Tag numbering

Tags are positive alphanumeric floating point numbers made of Latin letters and Arabic numerals that are used to identify and sort sections. Tagstrings are represented with an @code{@@} followed by the tag number. For example, @code{@@1.0}, @code{@@1.19}, @code{@@1.B2}, @code{@@12.5}, and @code{@@786.25} are valid tagstrings. The radix character @code{.} is used to separate the whole number from the fraction.

Floating point number, as explained before, allows inserting infinite number of tags between two pre-existing tags. We can also add tags indefinitely to the beginning and the ending of our document because there's always going to be a floating point number larger than the last tag number and a floating point number smaller than the first tag number.

It should be noted that, @code{@@1.1} is not a child of @code{@@1.0}. @code{@@1.0} is just a number larger than @code{@@1.0}. @code{@@1.0} is same as @code{@@1.0000000...} and @code{@@1.1} is same as @code{@@1.1000000...}.

Alphanumeric means the tag number is made out of both numbers @code{0} through @code{9} and alphabets @code{A} through @code{Z} and @code{a} through @code{z}. After @code{9} comes @code{A}. This is base 62 numeral system. This is chosen so we can express more tags with fewer digits. Furthermore, this allows creating more meaningful tagstrings such as @code{@@LogY2025.M04D15}.

All of the ``numbers'' in base 62 numeral system in ascending order:
@example
0123456789ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz
@end example

A tag section can have parent tag section and the tagstring will be expressed like @code{@@parent_tag/child_tag}. For example, @code{@@4.5/1.0}; here @code{@@1.0} is section under @code{@@4.5}. @code{@@4.5/1.0} is not the same as @code{1.0}. Both can coexist in the same document.

There a few rules to choosing a tag number. A tag number must be greater than zero. @code{@@0.0} is not a valid tagstring. A tagstring cannot contain anything other than one @code{@@} in the beginning, letters and digits for mantissa, one radix point @code{.} to separate the whole number from the fraction, and @code{/} seperators to create tags under tags. A tag section cannot have a higher tag number than the tag section after it and a tag section cannot have a lower tag number than the tag section before it. If a tag is inserted between two tags, it must be a number between those two tags.

Leading zeros may be ignored but it is adviced against unless it's right before the radic point @code{.}. Multiple radix points in one tagstring is allowed. It can be used for clarity. All the radix points other than the first one will be ignored. Similar to leading zeros, trailing zeros are also discouraged.

A tag number under the hood is implemented with a string mantissa and an integer exponent. This is similar to how floats and doubles are implemented. This custom data type is chosen over float/double for precision and infinite range.

@node Title
@section Title

A section or any other element can be given a title. For sections, the title is one word or multiple words inside curly braces that comes after the tagstring. Example of sections with titles:

@example
@@1.0 Title
@@2.0 @{Longer Title@}
@end example

@noindent A section must not be referenced using its title. The tagstring should be used instead. The title of a section can be used for description and exporting. Elements other than sections, such as files and variables, can be referred using their title.

@node References
@section References

References are important part of the Tag system. Using references we can make connections between two sections. This is useful when you have a section that refers to contents mentioned in another section. Futhermore, referencing allows us to use files and variables from another section and refer to files and variables to reuse them. Reference list comes after the title of a tag element and the underscore symbol @code{_}. If you are referencing only one element then you can write the address (tagstring or title depending on the element type) after the @code{_}. For example:

@example
@@1.0 @{Origin of Butterflies@}
::SOURCE: [[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10250192/]]
Butterflies are flying insects from the Lepidoptera
superfamily. Moleculars evidences suggests that
butterflies may have originated in the Cretaceous period.

@@2.0 @{Butterflies in Jurrassic Period?@} _ @@1.0
Butterflies likely originated from the Cretaceous
period, which was 145 to 66 million years ago.
Jurassic period was 201 and 145 million years ago.
So, unless there's new evidence, butterflies probably
didn't exist during the Jurassic period.
@end example

@noindent If you are referencing multiple elements then you'll have to put them inside comma separated curly braces. Example:

@example
@@3.75 World _ @{@@1.2, @@2.8, @@4.5@}
@end example

@noindent You can also refer to sections outside of the current document using either the absolute path or the relative path followed by @code{::} and the tagstring. For example:
@example
@@1.2 @{Why Tagging System@} _ @{./Notes.tag::@@1.0@}
@end example

@node Flags
@section Flags

Flags are words or labels used to convey the quality or features of a Tag element. For example, a section can have the ``ARCHIVED'' flag indicating that the section is now archived (and will be ignored by the compiler). Flag lists starts after the reference list followed by the caret sign @code{^}. If the reference list is absent, flag list can start right after the title. Flags are case sensitive. Here's an example of flag used to convey that a section is still incomplete:

@example
@@3.75 @{Faith and Humanity@} _ @@2.59 ^ incomplete
@end example

@noindent Flags can be used to convey that a section is now archived. For example:

@example
@@5.1 Drilldozer _ @@1.0 ^ ARCHIVED
@end example

@noindent Todo flags can be also used to turn a section into a todo item. ``TODO'' and ``DONE'' are the two basic todo states but more can be used. Examples of sections with todo flags:

@example
@@td.01 @{Finish section: attributes@} ^ TODO
@@td.02 @{Write about biggest overcomes@} ^ DONE
@@td.31 @{Share Tag with others@} ^ @{TODO, IMPORTANT@}
@end example

@noindent Some flags have parameter. For example, "MOVED-TO" flag will need an argument tagstring to indicate its new location. Example of a flag with argument that indicates the new location of the section:

@example
@@1.75 ^ MOVED-TO=@@5.0
@@5.0 Ingenuity
@end example

@noindent Flags aren't just labels you use to tell Tag compiler how you want your element to be treated. Flags can be words you use to describe a tag element. If a flag is foreign to the compiler, it will ignore it.

@node Contents
@section Contents

A section content is the body of the section. Contents may contain paragraphs, comments, metadata, etc. The content of a section starts right after the tagline and ends right before the next section's tagline or EOF. Example of a section with content:

@example
@@1.92 Title
This is the contents of section @@1.92 titled "Title".
@end example

@noindent Contents can have multiple paragraphs. Each paragraph will have to be separated by at least one blankline. For example:

@example
@@0.9A @{Multi-paragraph contents@}

It is possible for a section to have multiple paragraphs.

In that case they can be separated by blanklines.

Blanklines can also be used between taglines for clarity and readability.
@end example

It is possible for sections to not have any content at all. Example of two sections without contents:

@example
@@1.0 First
@@2.0 Second
@end example

@node Metadata
@section Metadata

Metadata are data about data. Metadata are useful when you need to provide information regarding certain data and how they should be processed. In Tag, metadata are used to tell the compiler how to process the data and provide context for human readers. Metadata resides in the content of a section and uses @code{::KEY:VALUE} format. Metadata can be used to indicate creation and modification time. For example:

@example
@@1.1 @{Section with creation time@}
::BIRTH: [2025-04-21T02:18:17+06:00]
::MODIFIED: [2025-04-21T21:44:52+06:00]
This section was created on 21st April 2025 at 2:18 and was
last modified on 21st April 2025 at 21:44 (UTC+06:00).
@end example

@noindent Metadata can be used to schedule a task. For example:

@example
@@9.47 @{Watch Le Trou (1960)@} ^ TODO
::SCHEDULED: <2025-06-01T18:00/PT2H20M>
@end example

@noindent Metadata entries that are situated at the beginning of the document and not part of any section are document metadata. They can tell the compiler how to process the whole document and also the viewer information about the whole documents. For example, metadata used to provide descriptive information:

@example
::TITLE: Metadata is amazing!
::AUTHOR: One who loves metadata
@@1.0 @{What is metadata@}
@@2.0 @{Why we need metadata@} ^ @@1.0
@end example

@noindent For some metadata a single line isn't enough. For such, we need metadata drawer. A metadata drawer is essentially a metadata set or schema. A metadata drawer starts with @code{:::DRAWER-NAME} and ends with @code{:::DRAWER-NAME}. Example of drawer used to keep log of all the changes of a section:

@example
@@4.7g2 @{Frequently changing section@}
:::LOGS
::MODIFIED: [2025-04-21T12:10:23Z]
::MODIFIED: [2025-04-21T12:05:47Z]
::MODIFIED: [2025-04-21T12:00:32Z]
::BIRTH: [2025-04-21T12:00:00Z]
:::LOGS
I last updated this section on 21st April 2025 at 12:10:23!
@end example

@noindent Drawers can used to keep a reference list. For example:

@example
@@2.15t @{Referencing is important!@} ^ markup

It is very important to reference what you cite. So, it is *important
to keep a list of all the references so you don't forget*.
Let's imagine that in this section we are citing the work of two imaginary
people Ivanka Ivanwich and Josephine Vargas. Ivanwish (1998) argues that
referencing is important for solely legality but Vargas (2003) points out
that there are other reasons referencing is important.

:::REFERENCES
Ivanka, I. (1998). /Laws and Library/. Damir Publishing.
Vargas, J. (2003). /Art of Academia/. Alba Arts.
:::REFERENCES
@end example

@noindent Feel free to make your own metadata for clarity and extension. A metadata key that is foreign to the compiler will be ignored.

@node Comments
@section Comments

Comments in Tag are special kind of metadata that can be used for annotation. A single line can be commented using the @code{::COMMENT:} key. For example:

@example
@@3.63 @{Provoking Thoughts@}
People of different philosophies have different ideas
on the origin of our thoughts and consciousness.
::COMMENT: Thoughts as a form of psychic energy
Thoughts in spirituality are believed to arise
from energy and vibration. Many spiritualists
also believe karma to be a by-product of thoughts.
@end example

@noindent A multi line comment can reside inside the @code{:::COMMENTS} drawer. For example:

@example
@@2.33 @{A lot to say@}
:::COMMENTS
Sometimes we have a lot to say about something
that may not fit in one line. In that case,
we can use the :::COMMENTS drawer.
:::COMMENTS
@end example

@noindent If you want your comments to be more cautionary then you can use warnings instead. Warnings are statements that warns the reader. They can can be done with @code{::WARNING:} metadata. For example:

@example
@@8.95 @{Liquid Methane and Hot Lava Demonstration@}
Let's find out what happens when hot lava meets liquid nitrogen.
::WARNING: DON'T TRY THIS AT HOME
We'll need rocks and enough heat to melt
them, liquid methane, and proper lab equipments.
@end example

@noindent Or, with the @code{:::WARNINGS} drawer. For example:

@example
@@3.423 @{Commenting taglines?@}
:::WARNINGS
Avoid putting taglines inside comments.
The behavior for doing so is undefined.
If you must, add a blank space at the
beginning of the commented tagline.
:::WARNING
@end example

